examples.question_text:
The graph shows a cubic $y=f(x)$ with a maximum at $x=-2$, a minimum at $x=2$ and an inflection at $x=0$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.75cm,y=0.12cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (-4,0)--(4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-21)--(0,23) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \a in {-2,2} {\draw[black] (\a,0.6)--(\a,-0.6);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\a,-0.6) {$\a$};}
\draw[black,thick,<->,smooth,domain=-3.8:3.8,samples=100,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{(\x)^3-12*\x+1});
\node[above] at (-2,17) {$y=f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
a. Sketch $y=f'(x)$ and $y=f''(x)$ on separate axes, showing their zeros and signs.
b. State the signs of $f'(-3)$ and $f''(-3)$.
c. State whether $f''(-2)$, $f(0)$ and $f'(2)$ are positive, negative or zero. Use the drawn concavity when deciding the sign of $f''(-2)$.

examples.solution_text:
a. The horizontal tangents give zeros of $f'$ at $x=-2$ and $x=2$. The cubic rises, falls, then rises, so $f'$ is positive, negative, then positive. Its graph is an upward-opening parabola.

The curve is concave down for $x<0$ and concave up for $x>0$. Thus $f''$ is negative, zero, then positive, and its graph is a rising line through the origin.

These sketches show the zeros and signs; numerical vertical scales are not required.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.75cm,y=0.3cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (-3.5,0)--(3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-5)--(0,6) node[above] {$f'(x)$};
\foreach \a in {-2,2} {\draw[black] (\a,0.15)--(\a,-0.15);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\a,-0.15) {$\a$};}
\draw[black,thick,<->,smooth,domain=-3:3,samples=80,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{(\x)^2-4});
\fill[black] (-2,0) circle (1.5pt);\fill[black] (2,0) circle (1.5pt);
\begin{scope}[yshift=-4.5cm]
\draw[black,->] (-3.5,0)--(3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-5)--(0,6) node[above] {$f''(x)$};
\draw[black,thick,<->] (-3,-4.5)--(3,4.5);
\node[below right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,0) {$0$};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
b. At $x=-3$ the curve rises and its gradient decreases, so $f'(-3)>0$ and $f''(-3)<0$.

c. The drawn curve has negative curvature at its maximum, so $f''(-2)<0$. Its $y$-intercept is above the axis, so $f(0)>0$. The tangent at the minimum is horizontal, so $f'(2)=0$.

examples.question_text:
The graph shows a quartic $y=f(x)$ with a horizontal inflection at $x=0$, a minimum at $x=4$, and a second inflection at $x=\frac83$. It has no other stationary or inflection points. Sketch $y=f'(x)$ and $y=f''(x)$, showing their zeros and signs.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.7cm,y=0.035cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (-1.8,0)--(6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-95)--(0,35) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \a/\lab in {2.666667/{\frac83},4/4} {\draw[black] (\a,2)--(\a,-2);\node[above,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\a,2) {$\lab$};}
\draw[black,thick,<->,smooth,domain=-1.3:5.5,samples=160,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.75*(\x)^4-4*(\x)^3});
\fill[black] (0,0) circle (1.5pt);
\node[above left] at (0,0) {$y=f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
$f$ decreases on both sides of $x=0$, with a horizontal tangent at $x=0$. Hence $f'$ touches the axis there from below. At $x=4$, $f$ changes from decreasing to increasing, so $f'$ crosses from negative to positive. Since $f$ is quartic, $f'$ is cubic.

The concavity of $f$ is up for $x<0$, down for $0<x<\frac83$, and up for $x>\frac83$. Thus $f''$ is an upward-opening parabola crossing the axis at $0$ and $\frac83$.

The sketches show qualitative vertical scales.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.7cm,y=0.18cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (-1.5,0)--(5.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-12)--(0,10) node[above] {$f'(x)$};
\node[below right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,0) {$0$};
\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (4,0) {$4$};
\draw[black,thick,<->,smooth,domain=-1.2:4.7,samples=120,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.5*(\x)^2*(\x-4)});
\fill[black] (0,0) circle (1.5pt);\fill[black] (4,0) circle (1.5pt);
\begin{scope}[yshift=-5cm]
\draw[black,->] (-1.5,0)--(5.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-5)--(0,10) node[above] {$f''(x)$};
\node[below left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,0) {$0$};
\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (2.666667,0) {$\frac83$};
\draw[black,thick,<->,smooth,domain=-1:3.7,samples=100,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{1.5*\x*(\x-8/3)});
\fill[black] (0,0) circle (1.5pt);\fill[black] (2.666667,0) circle (1.5pt);
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A horizontal inflection makes $f'$ touch the axis, while the change of concavity makes $f''$ cross it.

examples.question_text:
The graph shows $y=f'(x)$. It crosses the axis at $x=0$ and $x=4$, has a horizontal inflection at $x=0$, and has a minimum at $x=3$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.7cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (-2,0)--(5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-4)--(0,3.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \a in {3,4} {\draw[black] (\a,0.08)--(\a,-0.08);\node[above,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\a,0.08) {$\a$};}
\draw[black,thick,<->,smooth,domain=-1.5:4.3,samples=160,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{((\x)^4-4*(\x)^3)/8});
\draw[black,dashed] (3,0)--(3,-3.375);
\node[right] at (3,-3.375) {$y=f'(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
a. State where $f$ is increasing and decreasing.
b. Classify its stationary points.
c. Determine the $x$-coordinate of every inflection of $f$, explaining why a horizontal tangent on the drawn graph is not sufficient.

The graph shows $y=f''(x)$ for a twice-differentiable function $f$. It touches the axis at $x=-1$ and crosses it at $x=2$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.55cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (-3,0)--(3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-5)--(0,5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \a in {-1,2} {\draw[black] (\a,0.1)--(\a,-0.1);\node[above,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\a,0.1) {$\a$};}
\draw[black,thick,<->,smooth,domain=-2.5:2.7,samples=140,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.5*(\x+1)^2*(\x-2)});
\node[right] at (2.6,3.888) {$y=f''(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
a. State the concavity of $f$ on either side of each zero.
b. Identify the $x$-coordinates of its inflections.
c. Can you conclude that any inflection is horizontal?

examples.solution_text:
$f'(x)<0$ means the curve falls from left to right. $f''(x)>0$ means its gradient increases, becoming less negative. The curve is decreasing at a decreasing rate.

One possible sketch is:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(4.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,3.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \a in {1,4} {\draw[black] (\a,0.06)--(\a,-0.06);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\a,-0.06) {$\a$};}
\draw[black,thick,smooth,domain=1:4,samples=80,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{3/\x});
\fill[black] (1,3) circle (1.5pt);\fill[black] (4,0.75) circle (1.5pt);
\node[above right] at (2,1.5) {$y=f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The curve remains falling while becoming flatter.

examples.question_text:
The graph shows $f(x)=2e^{-x}$, which decreases and is concave up throughout its domain. Sketch $y=f'(x)$ and $y=f''(x)$ on the same axes, identifying each curve and its horizontal asymptote.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm,y=0.6cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (-1.5,0)--(4.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-0.5)--(0,6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,<->,smooth,domain=-1:4,samples=100,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2*exp(-\x)});
\draw[black] (-0.06,2)--(0.06,2);\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-0.06,2) {$2$};
\node[right] at (0.4,1.8) {$y=f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The negative gradients place $f'$ below the axis. As $f$ flattens towards $y=0$, its gradient approaches zero from below.

The positive concavity places $f''$ above the axis. For this exponential,
$\begin{align*}f'(x)&=-2e^{-x}\\f''(x)&=2e^{-x}.\end{align*}$
Both graphs approach the horizontal asymptote $y=0$ as $x\to\infty$. Neither has a zero.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm,y=0.5cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{mmred}{HTML}{EF6068}\definecolor{mmblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-1.5,0)--(4.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-6)--(0,6) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \a in {-2,2} {\draw[black] (-0.06,\a)--(0.06,\a);\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-0.06,\a) {$\a$};}
\draw[mmred,thick,<->,smooth,domain=-1:4,samples=100,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-2*exp(-\x)});
\draw[mmblue,thick,dashed,<->,smooth,domain=-1:4,samples=100,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2*exp(-\x)});
\node[mmred,right] at (1,-1.3) {$y=f'(x)$};
\node[mmblue,right] at (1,1.3) {$y=f''(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
The graph shows $f(x)=2\sin x$ for $0\le x\le2\pi$. Sketch $y=f''(x)$ on the same domain, and distinguish its zeros from the interior inflections of $f$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.75cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (-0.4,0)--(7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-2.6)--(0,2.7) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \a/\lab in {1.570796/{\frac\pi2},3.141593/\pi,4.712389/{\frac{3\pi}2},6.283185/{2\pi}} {\draw[black] (\a,0.08)--(\a,-0.08);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\a,-0.08) {$\lab$};}
\foreach \a in {-2,2} {\draw[black] (-0.06,\a)--(0.06,\a);\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-0.06,\a) {$\a$};}
\draw[black,thick,smooth,domain=0:6.283185,samples=150,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2*sin(deg(\x))});
\fill[black] (0,0) circle (1.5pt);\fill[black] (6.283185,0) circle (1.5pt);
\node[above] at (1.570796,2) {$y=f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
$f$ is concave down for $0<x<\pi$ and concave up for $\pi<x<2\pi$, so $f''$ is negative then positive.

Repeated differentiation gives
$\begin{align*}f'(x)&=2\cos x\\f''(x)&=-2\sin x.\end{align*}$
The graph of $f''$ is the reflection of the given graph in the $x$-axis. Its zeros are $0$, $\pi$ and $2\pi$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.75cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black,->] (-0.4,0)--(7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-2.6)--(0,2.7) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \a/\lab in {1.570796/{\frac\pi2},3.141593/\pi,4.712389/{\frac{3\pi}2},6.283185/{2\pi}} {\draw[black] (\a,0.08)--(\a,-0.08);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\a,-0.08) {$\lab$};}
\foreach \a in {-2,2} {\draw[black] (-0.06,\a)--(0.06,\a);\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-0.06,\a) {$\a$};}
\draw[black,thick,smooth,domain=0:6.283185,samples=150,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-2*sin(deg(\x))});
\fill[black] (0,0) circle (1.5pt);\fill[black] (3.141593,0) circle (1.5pt);\fill[black] (6.283185,0) circle (1.5pt);
\node[above] at (4.712389,2) {$y=f''(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Only $x=\pi$ is an interior inflection of $f$: the concavity changes there. The endpoints have no two-sided neighbourhood within the restricted domain.
